      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The RMSE and pattern correlation against WOA23 must be computed on one grid with one mask covering all three models and WOA23, applied to every field.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Put the nitracline maps on the WOA23 1° grid and build one mask over all four fields with `build_comparison_mask`. Apply it with `.where(mask)` before computing the RMSE and Pearson correlation, so all three models are scored on the same cells. Report the cell counts the mask records. WOA23 is the reference here, so the default `ocean_only=True` is right.",
        "Report `mean_data`, `mean_reference` and the bias from `compute_difference_metrics` over the shared sample. Global and basin means taken on each source's own grid are a different quantity. If they are differenced, state that the difference is not the bias on the shared mask."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The models come on native grids and the comparison is on the WOA23 1° grid, so regridding is only for maps and pattern comparisons, and the question's own areacello means native-grid handling matters for the mean statistics.",
      "status": "summary_fallback",
      "fallback": "the notes failed the check",
      "violations": [
        "number 35 is not in the fragment or the question"
      ],
      "rejected_notes": [
        "Compute the nitracline depth from the native-grid profiles, and take the global and basin mean depths there with each model's own `areacello`. Regrid to the WOA 1° grid only for the maps, the Pearson correlation and the RMSE. Use 'conservative' for `regrid_horizontal` and state the method with the quoted RMSE and correlation. Do not regrid the nitrate profiles first.",
        "The nitracline is the depth of the maximum gradient. It is quantised by the vertical grid and can jump between levels in the subtropical gyres, so read the maps together with the gradient. For comparison, put the models on a common level set with `regrid_vertical` rather than picking nearest levels. Note that GFDL-ESM4 is already remapped to 35 z levels."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-bgc-units-per-volume",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-bgc-units-per-volume.md",
      "sha256": "30d729792e1986dd91c822785512ceabe336ef10a47f0a7db602802e5425d70b",
      "reason": "Model no3 is per volume (mol m-3) and WOA23 nitrate is per mass (µmol/kg), so units must be reconciled for the Figure 2 shared colour scale and for any gradient comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Model `no3` is in mol m-3 (per volume), while WOA23 is in µmol kg-1 (per mass). The nitracline depth is scale-invariant, so take it from the native fields with no density conversion. Do not rescale by ×1000 and call the result µmol/kg.",
        "For Figure 2 the sections need one common unit. Converting the models to µmol kg-1 needs the in-situ density (`compute_potential_density_anomaly` with `ref_pressure=None`). Otherwise show the models in mmol m-3, and say that WOA23 is per mass and the models per volume. State the density used (`attrs['density_assumption']`)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "Basin means depend on the partition used. Here the WOA23 basin mask is used with marginal seas folded into their parent ocean and the Southern and Arctic Oceans excluded, and the result must say so.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Use the WOA23 basin mask through `select_basin` with `include_marginal_seas=True`, so marginal seas are folded into their parent ocean. Treat Southern and Arctic as separate basins and exclude them. Say that the mask follows WOA labelling and not the OMIP 10-region one.",
        "The mask labels each level separately, with marginal-sea labels ending at their sill. A nitracline depth is a 2-D quantity of the water column, so use the surface labels (`depth_dependent=False`). State this choice. Carry the same basin mask onto each model's grid."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "CESM2 and MPI are native gn (curvilinear) while GFDL-ESM4 is gr (regridded to 1° with 35 z levels), which affects the masks, the vertical levels used for the gradient, and how basin and global means are computed.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 is `gr`, already regridded to 1° (35 z levels, `conserve_order1`), so its \"native\" numbers are on that archive grid with a regridded land-sea mask. CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are `gn`. State this in the answer. MPI's coarser grid (250 km) also limits how well it resolves narrow features such as the equatorial Pacific."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "missing-values-and-land-cells",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "pitfalls/missing-values-and-land-cells.md",
      "sha256": "f576244f13dde06fed1777eaa9721c68844f61758f66fac704b44f1551e90df2",
      "reason": "Land and below-seafloor cells carry the 1e20 fill value, which would corrupt gradients and means in the nitracline search if CF masking is skipped.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build each model's valid mask from its own `no3` field, since masks differ between variables and models. Use NaN-aware reductions with weights masked to the same cells. Any value near 1e20 is an unmasked fill, not a result.",
        "Cells with no valid profile, or too few levels for a gradient, must stay NaN and not become 0 in the nitracline map. A zero would spread into neighbouring cells when the map is regridded."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/monthly-means-are-time-averages.md",
      "sha256": "df6b5c23460f88f1522e5c6a665e1efbb8322c3ae876c885530f3019aa284ee0",
      "reason": "Computing the nitracline from monthly means or from a climatological mean profile is an offline nonlinear diagnostic (a maximum gradient), and the answer must state the order of averaging used.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The nitracline depth is a nonlinear diagnostic (the depth of the maximum gradient), so the order of operations matters. Average the monthly `no3` to the 1971-2000 mean state first, because WOA23 is a climatology, then take the gradient. State this order. Average with `temporal_aggregate` (`freq='mean'`), which weights by month length. Do not use the equal-weight `compute_basic_stat(op='mean', dim='time')`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "The global and basin mean nitracline depths are area-weighted horizontal means, so the weighting by areacello matters.",
      "status": "dropped",
      "notes": [
        "Global and basin mean nitracline depths are area-weighted over valid cells only, using `compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'`. Use each model's own `areacello`, and the WOA 1° cell areas for WOA23. For the RMSE use `compute_difference_metrics` (area-weighted by default). For the pattern correlation use `analyze_correlation` with `weighting='area'`. An unweighted mean over a curvilinear or latitude grid is wrong."
      ]
    }
